// Theory [H01; source]: Separate statistical variation from systematic offsets; areal and
// peripheral variation contribute differently, with inverse-square-root area only in the
// areal-dominant regime.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §8.1;
// §8.2.1 Random Variation, Eqs8.6–8.10. Physical PDF pp. 368, 371–374.
// Applies when: Process/material/model coefficients and valid device dimensions must be
// characterized; differently sized units can be dominated by the smaller unit.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H01

// Theory [H02; source]: A resistor-network centroid is weighted by its sensitivity to unit
// resistance, rather than by cell outline or instance count.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §8.2.7
// Temperature, Eqs8.24–8.25. Physical PDF pp. 390.
// Applies when: Source example uses identical nominal resistors subject to equal absolute
// perturbation. A fractional perturbation generalization weights Ri·∂Req/∂Ri and is a chain-rule
// extension, not Eq8.24 verbatim.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H02

// Theory [H03; source]: Coincidence, symmetry, compactness and balanced subarrays reduce specified
// gradient errors; excessive segmentation can reduce active area and worsen random matching.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §8.2.7,
// Table8.4 and discussion following Table8.4. Physical PDF pp. 392–394.
// Applies when: Linear-field cancellation is first order. Curved fields, routing/end terms and
// fixed footprint require a separate error budget; no-gradient cases gain no gradient benefit.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H03

// Theory [H04; source]: For identical MOS sections with horizontal channel current, orientation is
// the mean of ±1 current directions. Matched arrays require equal signed means; equal nonzero
// means are allowed.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §13.2.5
// Gradients, Eq13.61 and Table13.2. Physical PDF pp. 708–709.
// Applies when: Identical unit sections and electrical terminal directions; in two dimensions
// compare both components. Relative geometric rotation alone is insufficient.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H04

// Theory [H05; source]: Long-channel saturation has ID=kVov²/2, gm=√(2kID), k=k′We/Le; the
// mismatch observable differs between fixed gate voltage and fixed drain current.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §12.1
// Eqs12.3–12.4; §12.1.1 Eqs12.6–12.9; §12.1.3 limits; §13.2 and §13.2.1. Physical PDF pp. 576–577,
// 584–585, 686–690.
// Applies when: Strong inversion, long-channel model; ignore CLM/velocity saturation only when
// justified. Use effective rather than blindly drawn geometry. Current/gate bias assumptions must
// accompany derived sensitivities.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H05

// Theory [H06; source]: Well implant scattering, trench stress and length-of-diffusion/end
// geometry can change otherwise nominally identical MOS devices.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §13.2.2
// Diffusion, Implantation, Etch, and Trench Effects; Figs13.49–13.52. Physical PDF pp. 695–698.
// Applies when: PDK-specific mechanism magnitude/sign and device polarity; preserve actual edge
// distances or qualified dummies. Fig13.52 is physical PDF698.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H06

// Theory [H07; source]: Matched MOS edges require suitable gate/dummy neighborhood and overhang;
// half/full dummies and inactive terminal ties are not interchangeable.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §13.2.2,
// Figs13.45–13.46; §13.3 dummy recommendations. Physical PDF pp. 692–693, 713–714.
// Applies when: Use qualified dummy extent/pitch and safe terminal bias. Added rectangles do not
// automatically reproduce the active device environment.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H07

// Theory [H08; source]: Metal above or near matched MOS regions can change
// hydrogenation/passivation and environmental symmetry.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §13.2.4
// Hydrogenation; §13.3 rules17–18. Physical PDF pp. 702–704, 716.
// Applies when: Process-dependent hydrogen transport/material stack; source numerical distances
// and preferences are not generic modern-node mandates. Fill also has extraction-dependent
// electrical effects. Handbook workflow implication: final fill must be included when rechecking
// affected geometry and electrical behavior; that lifecycle policy is our derivation from the
// physical mechanism.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H08

// Theory [H09; source]: Unequal widths turn common process bias into ratio error;
// contacts/heads/corners must be matched or modeled. An ideal equal-width right-angle corner is
// 0.5587 squares.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §6.2
// Resistor Layout, Eqs6.5–6.10; §8.2.2 Eq8.18. Physical PDF pp. 279–282, 374.
// Applies when: The corner result assumes the source ideal sheet/current-boundary geometry. Real
// contact and process-biased corners require calibrated models; identical systematic heads can
// cancel.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H09

// Theory [H10; source]: ET=SΔTC describes the net contact-temperature EMF; series sections can add
// or cancel it depending on electrical traversal. Common-centroid placement alone cannot cancel
// it.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §8.2.7,
// Eq8.34 and Figs8.22–8.23. Physical PDF pp. 401–402.
// Applies when: Matched material Seebeck behavior and temperature drops; opposite path directions
// must be paired. An even segment count alone does not guarantee cancellation in an arbitrary
// field.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H10

// Theory [H11; source]: Metal, contact and via contributions can change an intended resistor
// ratio; for ratioed devices each material contribution should preserve the ratio or be budgeted
// separately.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §8.2.4
// Interconnection Parasitics; §8.3.1 rule23. Physical PDF pp. 380–381, 422.
// Applies when: Different lead materials have different temperature coefficients. Equal body R
// ratios or equal route lengths alone do not establish terminal ratios.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H11

// Theory [H12; source]: Identical parallel units control ratio errors from area and periphery;
// series interconnection introduces different electrode-parasitic behavior.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §8.2.2
// Process Biases; §8.3.2 rules1–3. Physical PDF pp. 374–375, 423.
// Applies when: Fixed capacitor family and actual plate connections; nonunit geometries must match
// sensitivity rather than merely nominal C.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H12

// Theory [H13; source]: Capacitor electrodes can have asymmetric substrate/interconnect
// parasitics; edge dummies and shields need intentional quiet connections and balanced
// surroundings.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §7.1 MOS
// capacitor construction; §8.2.3 Fig8.5; §8.3.2 rules5–10. Physical PDF pp. 339–340, 378, 424–425.
// Applies when: Real terminal roles, finite shield impedance, bias and surrounding noise matter.
// Dummies cannot be assigned arbitrary floating or noisy potentials.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H13

// Theory [H14; source]: Vertical emitters, lateral emitter perimeters, beta/base-current errors,
// collector bias and thermal gradients demand different matching checks.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout.
// §10.2.1–3; §10.2.6; §10.3.1–2. Physical PDF pp. 506–509, 522–523, 526–529.
// Applies when: Identify matched observable and operating region; same total area does not make a
// lateral device equivalent, and reverse-BE stress can degrade accuracy.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H14

// Theory [H15; source]: PN current definition, Schottky field/barrier construction and nonuniform
// Zener avalanche produce different matching/injection behavior.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §11.2
// Schottky field-relief discussion; §11.3.1–3 diode matching. Physical PDF pp. 563, 565–570.
// Applies when: Schottky guard-junction forward bias can inject minority carriers; Zener segments
// may current-hog. Do not assume ordinary unit-area averaging applies to avalanche.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H15

// Theory [H16; source]: Plasma damage depends on the conductive geometry connected to a gate
// during each process stage; later-layer jumpers and protective diodes address different
// exposed-node situations.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §5.1.6
// The Antenna Effect. Physical PDF pp. 228–230.
// Applies when: Foundry defines gate denominator, exposed area/perimeter, layer accumulation and
// diode credit. Final net connectivity is insufficient; stage-graph implementation is handbook
// synthesis.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H16

// Theory [H17; source]: Collecting PN junctions, high-low blocking regions and majority taps have
// different functions; minority carriers can bypass shallow guards or debias weak returns.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §5.4.4
// Guard Rings; §14.2.1–5 process examples. Physical PDF pp. 264–270, 736–738, 741.
// Applies when: Qualified geometry/bias/substrate stack required. Blocking guards need appropriate
// complete 3D barriers; special collecting L/U guards near die edges are not generalized to
// blockers.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H17

// Theory [H18; source]: Clipping, snapback and rate-triggered protectors require different
// operating constraints; secondary protection must use the vulnerable device local reference and
// qualified limiting paths.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §14.4.1
// Primary Protection; §14.4.2 Secondary Protection, Fig14.36. Physical PDF pp. 750–752, 765–769.
// Applies when: Normal supply/slew/failsafe conditions, stress polarity and tested cell topology
// matter. Multifinger width does not imply uniform current; local ballast may be intentional.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H18

// Theory [H19; source]: Via arrays and bent leads need current-distribution analysis: inner-corner
// vias can carry far more current than outer ones.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §5.1.3
// Electromigration; §15.4.4, Eqs15.24–15.25 and Fig15.29. Physical PDF pp. 217–220, 821–822.
// Applies when: Foundry lifetime model, material/interface, local temperature and waveform
// determine limits. Total via count and average wire width are insufficient.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H19

// Theory [H20; source]: Shared-path voltage drops persist on one named net; capacitive disturbance
// depends on aggressor slew and victim impedance, while shields need an effective reference.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §15.4.4
// Special Routing Techniques, Figs15.26–15.28 and Eq15.23. Physical PDF pp. 818–820.
// Applies when: Use actual branch topology and local references. Eq15.23 is a simplified lumped
// relation; frequency-dependent impedances and return paths may require extraction.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H20

// Theory [H21; source]: Design-rule margins account for fabrication deviations; routing pitch must
// allow legal via/contact landing geometry, not only drawn line width plus nominal line spacing.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §3.2.3
// Design Rule Construction; §15.4.2 Eqs15.18–15.20; AppendixC disclaimer. Physical PDF pp.
// 138–144, 812–814, 836.
// Applies when: Numeric textbook rules are teaching examples. Use actual PDK shape/grid/stacking
// rules and qualified waivers; geometry needs to be checked after generation.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H21

// Theory [H22; derived]: For constant r,g and a singly terminated finger of length L,
// Rtotal=RSi·u·coth(u), u=L√(rg); small u gives added metal R≈rL/3.
// Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023
// catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §13.1.1 Eq13.11; AppendixE EqsE.1–E.4, E.7, E.9–E.13.
// Physical PDF pp. 650, 871–872.
// Applies when: Uniform geometry and distributed silicon conductance; v(0)=V, i(L)=0. Not a
// generic bound for tapered/strongly nonuniform devices. Supplied E.6 sign, E.8 prefactor and E.14
// sqrt appear inconsistent.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H22

// Theory [H23; source]: Skin/proximity loss, eddy currents, capacitance and surrounding metal
// determine frequency-dependent inductor quality; patterned shields must interrupt induced loops.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §7.2.1
// Inductor Parasitics and §7.2.2 Inductor Construction. Physical PDF pp. 358–363.
// Applies when: Use frequency-dependent port/EM models and qualified fill exclusions. Parallel
// metal stacking differs from series winding layers; more turns/width is not universally better.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H23

// Theory [H24; source]: Distributed gate resistance/capacitance causes nonuniform switching; body
// resistance and electrothermal feedback can localize current even when on-resistance has a
// positive temperature coefficient in another regime.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §13.1.2
// Switching Losses, Eqs13.21–13.23/Figs13.8–13.10; §13.1.3 SOA; §13.1.4 SenseFET discussion.
// Physical PDF pp. 652–655, 659–660, 667–668.
// Applies when: Waveform, bias, package heat path and process determine SOA. SenseFET layout must
// sample representative terminal voltages/temperature; repeated small-signal units alone do not
// establish power capability.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H24

// Theory [H25; source]: Heat flux follows Fourier’s law q=−κ∇T; package-dependent 3D heat flow and
// active-region weighting determine how temperature gradients affect matching.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §5.1.1
// Self-Heating, Eq5.6 finite source and Eqs5.7–5.9; §8.2.7 Temperature Eq8.22 and Eq8.23 context.
// Physical PDF pp. 213–214, 386–388.
// Applies when: Active-centroid model assumes local/planar contribution, suitable linear
// temperature response and a linear field. P/(2πκr) is not found verbatim in this book; a
// half-space point-source use must be labeled an independent Fourier-law derivation with its own
// boundaries.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#H25

// Theory [O01; source]: If pre-layout process values lie in [Pmin_proc,Pmax_proc] and every value
// receives the same shift dP, specification [L,U] requires L-Pmin_proc <= dP <= U-Pmax_proc.
// Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for
// Performance and Manufacturability. §2.2, Eqs. (2.1)–(2.7), printed pp.21–24. Physical PDF pp.
// 34–37.
// Applies when: A common additive shift; use scenario-specific response otherwise.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O01

// Theory [O02; source]: Independent allocations of per-parasitic limits can exclude electrically
// acceptable combinations; direct performance constraints preserve those tradeoffs.
// Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for
// Performance and Manufacturability. §2.3.3 and §2.4; Fig.2.4 and direct-performance discussion.
// Physical PDF pp. 40–42.
// Applies when: The response model and specifications define feasibility; a linearized region is
// only local.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O02

// Theory [O03; source]: dPj is approximated by sum_i Sji dXi. An adjoint computes derivatives with
// respect to many parameters for one scalar response.
// Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for
// Performance and Manufacturability. §2.4.1 Eqs. (2.12)–(2.13); §2.4.2.3 Eqs. (2.18)–(2.24).
// Physical PDF pp. 42–44.
// Applies when: Differentiable local response; suitable simulator convergence; independent
// responses generally need independent adjoints.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O03

// Theory [O04; source]: A uniform rectangular segment has R=Rsheet L/W; overlap, lateral and
// fringing capacitance have distinct geometry-dependent contributions.
// Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for
// Performance and Manufacturability. §2.5.3.1 Eqs. (2.29)–(2.32); §2.5.3.2 Eq. (2.33), printed
// pp.36–38. Physical PDF pp. 49–51.
// Applies when: Calibrated material/layer/environment; L and W use the same units; contacts/vias
// and nonuniform current need additional modeling.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O04

// Theory [O05; source]: The cited pair model is variance=Aq^2/(WL)+Sq^2 D^2, with
// structure/orientation/environment constraints used alongside it.
// Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for
// Performance and Manufacturability. §2.7.1 Eq. (2.35); §2.7.2 matching guidelines. Physical PDF
// pp. 53–55.
// Applies when: Process-calibrated pair model and its geometry domain; coefficients are not
// universal foundry constants; common centroid does not remove the finite-area random term.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O05

// Theory [O06; source]: Position, orientation and shape relationships are distinct; coordinated
// translations, swaps, flips and axis movement can preserve chosen relationships by construction.
// Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for
// Performance and Manufacturability. §§4.6–4.7: constraint groups and translation, swap, flip and
// symmetry-axis moves. Physical PDF pp. 99–105.
// Applies when: Compatible group membership, fixed-object conditions and legal variants;
// preserving one relationship does not prove neighbor clearance or electrical matching.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O06

// Theory [O07; source]: For fixed energy and symmetric proposals, uphill acceptance is exp(-dE/T);
// T0≈-mean(positive dE)/ln(p0) is an initial calibration heuristic.
// Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for
// Performance and Manufacturability. §4.4.6 Metropolis criterion, PDF89–90; §4.11 Eqs.
// (4.37)–(4.38), PDF117–118. Physical PDF pp. 89–90, 117–118.
// Applies when: T>0, 0<p0<1, consistent energy units, finite search; exp of mean does not equal
// mean acceptance. No finite-run global optimum guarantee.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O07

// Theory [O08; source]: Required wire width and via count depend on current carried by each route
// segment and on process current capability.
// Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for
// Performance and Manufacturability. §5.6.2 Eqs. (5.1)–(5.2); §5.7.1 Eq. (5.9). Physical PDF pp.
// 143–147.
// Applies when: Current scenario and branch topology must be known; waveform, temperature and
// current sharing require the relevant reliability model.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O08

// Theory [O09; source]: For long parallel wires with length L and spacing s, bridging critical
// area is L max(0,chi-s); expected faults integrate critical area against defect density, with
// Poisson no-fault probability exp(-lambda).
// Source: Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for
// Performance and Manufacturability. §5.9.1 Eqs. (5.16)–(5.18), Eq. (5.24), Fig.5.15; printed
// pp.141–143. Physical PDF pp. 153–155.
// Applies when: chi is defect diameter; simplified long-wire geometry omits detailed end effects;
// density and Poisson assumptions must be calibrated; not parametric mismatch yield.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O09

// Theory [O10; source]: For one common vertical symmetry axis, pos_alpha(x)<pos_alpha(y) iff
// pos_beta(sym(y))<pos_beta(sym(x)) is a sufficient symmetric-feasible sequence-pair condition,
// not a necessary condition for physical symmetry.
// Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological
// Approaches. Balasa, Chapter1 §1.3, Eq. (1.1); sufficient-not-necessary discussion PDF50.
// Physical PDF pp. 48–51.
// Applies when: Source sequence convention, distinct group members, reflected partner mapping;
// multiple groups need compatible embedding (§1.3.2 PDF60–61).
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O10

// Theory [O11; source]: A symmetry island is an abutting connected group. ASF-B-star stores
// representative half-groups and reconstructs counterparts; self-symmetric representatives touch
// the axis.
// Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological
// Approaches. Lin and Chang, §§2.2.2–2.3.1, Definition2.1, Lemma2.1 and Property2.1. Physical PDF
// pp. 79–85.
// Applies when: Legal module envelopes and chapter-specific B-star convention;
// connectivity/abutment is stronger than geometric symmetry.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O11

// Theory [O12; source]: The DTI example permits a shared range or a sufficiently isolated range,
// excludes intermediate gaps, and limits a middle device total left-plus-right stretch.
// Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological
// Approaches. Strasser et al., §3.1.2 Fig.3.3; §3.3.2 Eqs. (3.36)–(3.40). Physical PDF pp. 115,
// 135–137.
// Applies when: Technology-specific DTI semantics and finite geometry bounds; not every isolation
// rule has these branches.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O12

// Theory [O13; source]: Ordinary horizontal composition gives (w1+w2,max(h1,h2)); enhanced shapes
// retain B-star topology and can interlock contours. Hierarchical enumeration does not enumerate
// all circuit layouts.
// Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological
// Approaches. Strasser et al., §§3.4–3.5, Figs.3.23–3.33, Algorithm3.4. Physical PDF pp. 137–146.
// Applies when: Fixed hierarchy and variants; composition constraint-preservation assumes no
// incompatible added parent constraints; geometric dominance is not automatically electrical
// dominance.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O13

// Theory [O14; source]: An acyclic fixed-order graph solves xj-xi>=dij by longest paths; symmetry
// relations involving sums/differences of more variables require a richer linear representation.
// Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological
// Approaches. Said et al., §5.4.1 Eqs. (5.1)–(5.2); §5.4.2 Eq. (5.6); §5.5.1 Definition5.2.
// Physical PDF pp. 219–229.
// Applies when: One-dimensional fixed ordering with appropriate graph feasibility; LP optimality
// concerns the stated model, not all topology choices or nonlinear electrical effects.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O14

// Theory [O15; source]: Each sizing can select geometric parameters, instantiate or model layout
// parasitics, and evaluate the resulting circuit. A naive table with m samples in n dimensions has
// m^n entries.
// Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological
// Approaches. Castro-López et al., §6.3.3 flow; §6.4.2 shape propagation; §6.5.1 extraction
// approaches A/B; Table6.7. Physical PDF pp. 263, 267–268, 272–275.
// Applies when: Fixed characterized template/technology/model domain; sensitivity screening
// reduces sampling but cannot certify omitted parasitics in all future contexts.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O15

// Theory [O16; source]: Management, derivation, transformation, sensitivity analysis and
// verification are distinct services; static final verification uses fixed design and constraint
// data.
// Source: Helmut E. Graeb, editor · 2011. Analog Layout Synthesis: A Survey of Topological
// Approaches. Jerke, Lienig and Freuer, §7.4, Fig.7.4; §§7.4.1–7.4.5. Physical PDF pp. 285–293.
// Applies when: Applicable rules and model coverage must be defined; DRC/LVS alone do not express
// all analog intent; transformations can restrict the remaining design space.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O16

// Theory [O17; source]: Yield is probability over statistical fabrication variables that every
// specified performance bound holds for every operating-range condition.
// Source: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing. §§3.1–3.5 parameter classes;
// §4.8.1 Eq. (114), §§4.8.2–4.8.4. Physical PDF pp. 46–50, 89–95.
// Applies when: Declared joint statistical distribution and complete range contract; a sampled
// operating point or process-corner list alone is a different analysis.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O17

// Theory [O18; source]: For local response f≈f0+S(x-mu), Cov(f)≈S C S-transpose; a scalar
// sensitivity column g gives variance g-transpose C g.
// Source: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing. §5.2 Eq. (175); AppendixA.9
// Eqs. (A.13) and (A.15). Physical PDF pp. 111, 183.
// Applies when: Finite second moments; exact for affine response, approximate for nonlinear
// response. C must be PSD, inverse whitening requires positive definiteness or a reduced-rank
// subspace.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O18

// Theory [O19; source]: For delta-transpose C-inverse delta<=beta^2 and f=f0+g-transpose delta,
// extrema occur at delta=±beta Cg/sqrt(g-transpose Cg) and f=f0±beta sqrt(g-transpose Cg).
// Source: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing. §5.2.1–5.2.2, Eqs.
// (171)–(179), printed p.93. Physical PDF pp. 111.
// Applies when: Positive-definite C, beta>=0 and g-transpose Cg>0; zero gradient/variance requires
// separate treatment; nonlinear circuits need validated approximation.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O19

// Theory [O20; source]: A standardized n-dimensional Gaussian radius-beta ball encloses
// P(chi-square_n<=beta^2); a symmetric independent box encloses [2Phi(beta)-1]^n; one linear upper
// bound has probability Phi(beta).
// Source: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing. §§6.2.2–6.2.4, Eq. (240),
// Eqs. (242)–(248), Eqs. (258)–(259), Tables11–12. Physical PDF pp. 133–143.
// Applies when: Independent standardized coordinates for box product; positive signed margin for
// usual passing example; joint circuit yield is an intersection, not automatically any of these
// regions.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O20

// Theory [O21; derived]: With N independent Bernoulli trials and no failures, a one-sided 95%
// upper failure-probability bound is 1-0.05^(1/N); ordinary Monte Carlo yield variance is
// Y(1-Y)/N.
// Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design
// Centering and Sizing. §6.1.1 Eq. (219), §6.1.3 Eqs. (225)–(226); confidence limit is derived
// here. Physical PDF pp. 125–132.
// Applies when: Independent, identically distributed complete pass/fail events; not the variance
// formula for weighted importance samples or unresolved simulation outcomes.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O21

// Theory [O22; source]: For Gaussian process covariance fixed and acceptance region fixed,
// gradient_mu Y=Y C-inverse(mu_accepted-mu). A stationary positive-yield point matches the
// accepted and original means.
// Source: Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design Centering and Sizing. §7.1.2, Eq. (324), printed
// pp.145–146. Physical PDF pp. 163–164.
// Applies when: Unconstrained mean variation, fixed covariance and fixed region; necessary
// stationarity only. Width/topology changes normally alter the region and cannot be substituted
// without chain rules.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O22

// Theory [O23; derived]: With residual e in R^m and row-gradient Jacobian J in R^(m by n), the
// regularized linear least-squares step satisfies (J-transpose J+lambda I)r=-J-transpose e.
// Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design
// Centering and Sizing. §7.2.3 Eq. (362) and printed Eqs. (365),(368); printed pp.158–159.
// Physical PDF pp. 175–176.
// Applies when: Local residual linearization, lambda>=0 and suitable solve/rank treatment; source
// RHS omits transpose, verified in actual raster. Not a global circuit-optimum certificate.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O23

// Theory [O24; derived]: For L=f-lambda(beta^2-delta-transpose C-inverse delta), Hessian_delta
// L=Hessian f+2lambda C-inverse; covariance curvature alone does not establish nonlinear
// second-order sufficiency.
// Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Helmut E. Graeb · 2007. Analog Design
// Centering and Sizing. §5.4 Eq. (189), prose following Eq. (191); AppendixC.6 Eq. (C.24).
// Physical PDF pp. 115, 195.
// Applies when: Twice differentiable f, constant positive-definite C and fixed multiplier for
// Hessian; constrained sufficiency concerns appropriate feasible/critical directions. The prose
// omission was verified in raster.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O24

// Theory [O25; derived]: For the difference of weighted responses to a shared affine spatial
// field, equal zeroth and first moments cancel that field. Ratio comparisons first normalize each
// group by its intended total; equal raw device counts are not necessary.
// Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Koen Lampaert, Georges Gielen and
// Willy Sansen · 1999. Analog Layout Generation for Performance and Manufacturability. §2.7.2
// common-centroid motivation; contrasting geometric area-weighted group center in Topological
// §3.1.1 Eq. (3.12), PDF112. Physical PDF pp. 53–55.
// Applies when: Same local field and proportional unit response; comparison weights encode the
// intended ratio. Second-order fields, unequal environments and routing bias are not canceled by
// first moments alone.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#O25

// Theory [P01; source]: For the characterized equal-device pair model, variance is approximated by
// A_P²/(WL)+S_P²D².
// Source: Marcel J. M. Pelgrom and Aad C. J. Duinmaijer · ESSCIRC 1988. Matching properties of MOS
// transistors. Analysis, Eq.(1), printed pp.327–328. Physical PDF pp. 1–2.
// Applies when: Many small additive local events with correlation distance much smaller than
// device dimensions, plus a fitted long-range component; process/parameter-specific coefficients.
// A refers to the pair-difference statistic here. A single-device coefficient requires its own
// convention conversion.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P01

// Theory [P02; source]: Current-factor variance contains width-edge, length-edge, mobility and
// oxide terms before an approximate A_beta²/(WL) reduction.
// Source: Marcel J. M. Pelgrom and Aad C. J. Duinmaijer · ESSCIRC 1988. Matching properties of MOS
// transistors. Matching of MOS transistors, Eq.(3), printed p.328. Physical PDF pp. 2.
// Applies when: Area-dependent contribution only; same defined process parameters; authors say
// simplification becomes less valid as W and L decrease.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P02

// Theory [P03; source]: The studied 90-degree rotation affected current-factor mismatch and varied
// by wafer/batch; this experiment did not establish arbitrary transform equivalence.
// Source: Marcel J. M. Pelgrom and Aad C. J. Duinmaijer · ESSCIRC 1988. Matching properties of MOS
// transistors. Orientation discussion, Fig.3(d), printed pp.328–330. Physical PDF pp. 2–4.
// Applies when: 2.5µm n-well CMOS experiment; no universal rotational/reflection/source-drain
// symmetry rule for another process.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P03

// Theory [P04; source]: Treating threshold and gain-factor mismatch as independent can double
// count an underlying oxide-thickness fluctuation; bias and geometry matter.
// Source: Patrick G. Drennan and Colin C. McAndrew · JSSC 2003. Understanding MOSFET mismatch for
// analog design. Section II, Table I and discussion surrounding Eqs.(4)–(6), printed p.451.
// Physical PDF pp. 2.
// Applies when: Do not apply this critique as a prohibition on all calibrated local empirical
// fits; inspect whether the statistical basis and correlations are already handled.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P04

// Theory [P05; derived]: For a first-order expansion Δe≈JΔp, Var(e)≈J C Jᵀ; Drennan Eq.(8) is the
// independent-parameter special case.
// Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Patrick G. Drennan and Colin C.
// McAndrew · JSSC 2003. Understanding MOSFET mismatch for analog design. Section II, Eqs.(7)–(11),
// printed p.452. Physical PDF pp. 3.
// Applies when: Small perturbations around the specified bias/geometry, valid compact model, and
// covariance C for the same physical variables; nonlinear tails need additional treatment.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P05

// Theory [P06; derived]: In a long-channel saturation, threshold-only toy model at fixed current,
// relative current variance from threshold mismatch is proportional to 1/L² and independent of W.
// Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Patrick G. Drennan and Colin C.
// McAndrew · JSSC 2003. Understanding MOSFET mismatch for analog design. Eq.(9) and §III.A
// Geometry and Bias Interrelationship, printed pp.452,454. Physical PDF pp. 3, 5.
// Applies when: This is an illustrative derivation, not a modern compact-model law; ignore
// mobility/edge/series/short-channel contributions and use the stated area coefficient. Recheck
// headroom.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P06

// Theory [P07; source]: Four independent 20µm-wide units can reproduce the ideal mismatch of one
// 80µm-wide device at the same length; there is no free fixed-total-width averaging gain.
// Source: Patrick G. Drennan and Colin C. McAndrew · JSSC 2003. Understanding MOSFET mismatch for
// analog design. §III.C Multiple-Unit Devices, printed p.455. Physical PDF pp. 6.
// Applies when: Width effects and altered electrical/parasitic context neglected; compare equal
// total width, not an array with one small unit. Circuit-role differences are separately shown in
// Table III.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P07

// Theory [P08; source]: Injected substrate current paths depend on injector, receiver, other
// contacts, doping profile and backplate potential; taps/wells may inject return noise.
// Source: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto
// Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization
// for IC Design. §1.1 and Chapter 2 §§2–3, printed pp.2,15–17. Physical PDF pp. 25, 38–40.
// Applies when: State injector mechanisms, reference connections and receiving impedance/body
// bias; a distance-only pair model omits third-contact loading.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P08

// Theory [P09; source]: Conductive and displacement currents obey ∇·(σ∇φ)+∂t[∇·(ε∇φ)]=0; the
// resistive limit and explicit contact/edge/interface boundaries determine the model.
// Source: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto
// Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization
// for IC Design. Chapter 3 §2 Electromagnetic Formulation, Eqs.(3.1)–(3.3), printed pp.22–23.
// Physical PDF pp. 45–46.
// Applies when: Electroquasistatic continuum regime. Resistive approximation requires ε/σ small
// relative to times of interest; contact equipotential and insulating/grounded boundaries must
// match the physical setup.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P09

// Theory [P10; source]: The book defines contact isolation as V_receiver/V_injector; it depends on
// contact capacitance, backside impedance, frequency and receiver load, not only substrate
// resistance.
// Source: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto
// Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization
// for IC Design. Chapter 8 §2 Characterizing Isolation and §4 Effects of Load Impedance, printed
// pp.101–102,111–112. Physical PDF pp. 124–125, 134–135.
// Applies when: Use a specified source/reference/loading convention. A negative-dB transfer and a
// positive attenuation magnitude have opposite reported signs.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P10

// Theory [P11; source]: Finite backplate impedance can make isolation approximately independent of
// further contact separation in the book’s low-resistivity examples.
// Source: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto
// Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization
// for IC Design. §8.2.1 Low-Resistivity Substrates, Figs.8.6–8.7; contrast §8.2.2, printed
// pp.102–107. Physical PDF pp. 125–127, 130.
// Applies when: Depends on epitaxial profile, area, frequency and backplate connection. Do not use
// its multiple-of-epitaxial-thickness example as a universal spacing rule.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P11

// Theory [P12; source]: A ring can worsen received noise through finite/noisy grounding; internal
// versus external signal reference and ring width/location change the result.
// Source: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto
// Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization
// for IC Design. §8.6.1 Grounding Schemes and §8.6.2 Optimization of Guard Ring Widths,
// Figs.8.16–8.23, printed pp.116–124. Physical PDF pp. 139–140, 142–147.
// Applies when: The numerical studies use specific high-resistivity substrates and lumped package
// models. Compare actual reference voltage and include ring-return inductance and pickup.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P12

// Theory [P13; source]: Conductive shields provide an alternate current return, dielectric
// isolation raises impedance, and buried opposite-type wells combine junction isolation with
// conductive shielding.
// Source: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto
// Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization
// for IC Design. §8.9 Buried Substrate Shields, Figs.8.27–8.30, printed pp.128–133. Physical PDF
// pp. 151–156.
// Applies when: The process must support the structure and bias. Conductive return impedance,
// finite shield resistivity, junction capacitance and frequency limit isolation.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P13

// Theory [P14; derived]: In a linear receiver difference, v_diff=(H1−H2)v_aggressor; equal
// common-mode transfer cancels only while the two complete paths and receiver responses match.
// Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey,
// Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook.
// Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization for IC Design. §8.3 Improving Isolation Using
// Differential Circuits, Figs.8.9–8.10, printed pp.107–111. Physical PDF pp. 130–134.
// Applies when: Complex transfer functions with common reference. The source explicitly studies a
// 5% receiving-capacitor mismatch; phase, loading and circuit mismatch must remain included.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P14

// Theory [P15; source]: Local noise generators permit performance-to-parasitic budget generation;
// the PLL example shows jitter depends on the phase of injection relative to transitions.
// Source: Edoardo Charbon, Ranjit Gharpurey, Paolo Miliozzi, Robert G. Meyer and Alberto
// Sangiovanni-Vincentelli · 2001 print / 2003 ebook. Substrate Noise: Analysis and Optimization
// for IC Design. Chapter 5 §§1–3, Eqs.(5.1)–(5.5), §5.3 case study, printed pp.47–53,58. Physical
// PDF pp. 70–73, 75–76, 81.
// Applies when: Local linearization or explicitly maximized sensitivity over a defined interval;
// source waveform/phase assumptions and circuit operating state must be retained. Shared budget
// allocation is not unique.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P15

// Theory [P16; source]: Electrical, thermal and stress driving forces produce atomic flux;
// accumulation/depletion and coupled heating/stress determine vulnerable regions.
// Source: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware
// Integrated Circuit Design. §2.2 and §2.5, especially Eqs.(2.15)–(2.18), printed pp.18,35–43.
// Physical PDF pp. 30, 47–48, 52–55.
// Applies when: Material paths, interface boundary conditions, temperature and mechanical state
// matter; average current density alone is not a damage-location model.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P16

// Theory [P17; source]: t50=C j^-n exp(Ea/kT) is a generalized empirical median-lifetime relation;
// a product reliability target also needs mission profile and failure statistics.
// Source: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware
// Integrated Circuit Design. Eq.(2.1), generalized Eq.(3.8), §§3.4.1–3.4.2, printed
// pp.18–19,79–86. Physical PDF pp. 30–31, 91–98.
// Applies when: Same characterized process, geometry, mechanism, temperature/current range and
// failure criterion. Median is not low-failure-probability lifetime of every segment; do not
// extrapolate across mechanism changes.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P17

// Theory [P18; source]: RMS, effective/directional average and peak currents correspond to
// different mechanisms; all applicable dimensioning limits must be considered.
// Source: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware
// Integrated Circuit Design. §2.4.3 and §3.3.1 Current Types, printed pp.29–32,70–71. Physical PDF
// pp. 41–44, 82–83.
// Applies when: RMS heating assumes resistance treated consistently; self-healing depends on
// characterized waveform/frequency/material behavior. Zero signed average does not imply zero
// damage.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P18

// Theory [P19; source]: Signed terminal-current bounds/scenarios determine tree segment demand;
// current-driven planning can minimize required interconnect area rather than only length.
// Source: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware
// Integrated Circuit Design. §§3.3.2–3.3.3, Eqs.(3.1)–(3.7), Figs.3.4–3.10, printed pp.71–78.
// Physical PDF pp. 83–90.
// Applies when: For transient cut sums include relevant interconnect shunt currents; retain
// mode/correlation assumptions. Meshes require a network current-sharing solution. The handbook’s
// exact waveform sum is a KCL expression, distinct from the book’s equivalent-current bounds.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P19

// Theory [P20; source]: Width must meet the largest applicable current/manufacturing requirement;
// pin landing regions and internal pin geometry need ampacity verification.
// Source: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware
// Integrated Circuit Design. §§3.5.1–3.5.3, Eqs.(3.21)–(3.24), Fig.3.15, printed pp.87–90.
// Physical PDF pp. 99–102.
// Applies when: Application-robust per-layer rules, conducting thickness/width corrections and
// actual terminal current. mA/µm and A/cm² are distinct rule conventions. Via-temperature
// Eq.(3.25) requires the separate caveat in P22.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P20

// Theory [P21; source]: Via arrays at direction changes can overload inner cuts; equal cut count
// is not equal maximum-cut utilization.
// Source: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware
// Integrated Circuit Design. §4.6 Multiple Vias, especially §4.6.4 and Figs.4.25–4.29, printed
// pp.122–128. Physical PDF pp. 134–140.
// Applies when: Include both connected metal layers, contact resistances and reservoir/mechanical
// consequences. The source’s geometric equalization examples are conditional templates, not
// universal equal-sharing proofs.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P21

// Theory [P22; derived]: For unchanged Black parameters and target lifetime,
// j_allow(T)/j_allow(Tref)=exp[(Ea/(nk))(1/T−1/Tref)]. Required identical-via count grows as the
// inverse capacity factor.
// Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele ·
// 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware Integrated Circuit Design. Derived from Eq.(3.8);
// compare printed Eqs.(3.25)–(3.26), printed pp.81,89. Physical PDF pp. 93, 101.
// Applies when: Absolute Kelvin temperatures, positive Ea and n, unchanged geometry/failure
// mechanism. Printed via-count equations exhibit apparent inverse-factor inconsistency; no
// publisher erratum or foundry qualification was established.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P22

// Theory [P23; source]: The one-dimensional balance gives |jL|crit=ΩΔσ/(e|Z*|ρ), subject to
// confinement, boundary and failure assumptions; void-growth saturation is a distinct criterion.
// Source: Jens Lienig and Matthias Thiele · 2018. Fundamentals of Electromigration-Aware
// Integrated Circuit Design. §4.3, Eq.(4.1), discussion of Eq.(4.2); §4.5.1 neighboring segments,
// printed pp.105–108,111,115,118. Physical PDF pp. 117–120, 123, 127, 130.
// Applies when: Physical boundaries and material flow, not arbitrary route vertices. Simple
// isolated segment model; branched/reservoir structures and transients require additional
// analysis. A safe-looking steady state is not proof of a damage-free transition.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P23

// Theory [P24; derived]: For a surface point heater in a homogeneous isotropic semi-infinite body
// with an otherwise adiabatic surface and fixed far-field temperature, ΔT=P/(2πkr).
// Background/derivation basis (not the exact derived rule): Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023
// catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. Fourier-law basis: §8.2.7 Thermal Gradients,
// Eq.(8.22), printed p.385. The half-space result is derived in the handbook, not printed as this
// equation in the book.. Physical PDF pp. 386.
// Applies when: Constant k, steady state, hemispherical spreading, ideal point source, no finite
// die/package/layer effects. Singular near-source field requires finite-source treatment or
// labeled regularization.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P24

// Theory [P25; source]: A device’s average temperature equals its active-area centroid temperature
// only under the stated active-area, equal-contribution, linear electrical-temperature and linear
// spatial-temperature assumptions.
// Source: Alan Hastings · third edition · 2023 catalog / ©2024. The Art of Analog Layout. §8.2.7
// Centroids, followed by Eq.(8.23), printed pp.386–387. Physical PDF pp. 387–388.
// Applies when: Package and power conditions determine the field. Unequal electrical weighting,
// finite nonlinear fields or nonlinear device temperature dependence require more detailed
// evaluation. The handbook’s general Σs_i ΔT_i is a first-order sensitivity extension.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#P25

// Theory [Q01; source]: Coincident normalized x/y centroids remove linear spatial parameter
// mismatch; translating parameter mismatch into performance uses a separate sensitivity
// approximation.
// Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin
// and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and
// Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead. Eqs.(1)–(5), printed p.114. Physical PDF pp. 1.
// Applies when: Equal-unit or explicitly normalized additive responses; shared parameter field;
// circuit linearization valid locally.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q01

// Theory [Q02; source]: WPE, LOD, OD dimensions and gate pitch can shift device parameters even
// when centroids coincide.
// Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin
// and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and
// Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead. §II-A2/Fig.2 and §V-A/Fig.9, printed pp.115,119.
// Physical PDF pp. 2, 6.
// Applies when: Relevant process/LDE model; review’s identical-unit simplifications presume its
// row, pitch and diffusion-sharing construction.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q02

// Theory [Q03; source]: The OTA/comparator cases show circumstances in which
// clustered/interdigitated patterns outperform some CC structures.
// Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin
// and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and
// Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead. §V, Figs.9–10, printed pp.119–120. Physical PDF
// pp. 6–7.
// Applies when: Reported device/circuit/process assumptions; not a universal CC prohibition or a
// portable size threshold.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q03

// Theory [Q04; source]: Half-diffusion graphs/Euler paths express compatible sharing;
// source-current distribution and route resistance affect array transconductance.
// Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin
// and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and
// Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead. §IV; §V-A Eq.(25)/Fig.9, printed pp.118–120.
// Physical PDF pp. 5–7.
// Applies when: Keep actual net labels and unit paths; generalized routing acceptance is a
// handbook adaptation.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q04

// Theory [Q05; source]: MOM finger/sandwich/pillar/mortise-tenon constructions trade density and
// terminal coupling.
// Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Meghna Madhusudan, Arvind K. Sharma, Ramesh Harjani, Mark Po-Hung Lin
// and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · ASP-DAC 2022, pp. 114–121. Common-Centroid Layout for Active and
// Passive Devices: A Review and the Road Ahead. §III-B, Fig.5, printed p.117. Physical PDF pp. 4.
// Applies when: A source-specific advanced-node comparison; selecting a qualified PDK primitive
// still requires its own model.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q05

// Theory [Q06; source]: Under an additive identical-unit parameter model, equal spatial moments
// through n cancel every polynomial component of degree at most n.
// Source: Xin Dai, Chengming He, Hanqing Xing, Degang Chen and Randall Geiger · ISCAS 2005. An
// Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for nonlinear gradients cancellation. §II
// Eqs.(4)–(6); §III Eqs.(15)–(23), printed pp.4836–4837. Physical PDF pp. 2–3.
// Applies when: Two identically designed devices; parameter additivity; unit center approximates
// area average, or finite-footprint equivalence is independently justified.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q06

// Theory [Q07; source]: Reflect the prior pattern, retaining labels for odd target order and
// exchanging labels for even target order; the specified base leads to 2^n units per device.
// Source: Xin Dai, Chengming He, Hanqing Xing, Degang Chen and Randall Geiger · ISCAS 2005. An
// Nth-order central symmetrical layout pattern for nonlinear gradients cancellation. §III
// recursive construction and induction, printed pp.4836–4837. Physical PDF pp. 2–3.
// Applies when: First-order base has two units per device; legal disjoint placement; not a
// minimal-count or circuit-performance theorem.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q07

// Theory [Q08; source]: Binary and split DAC networks respond differently to top/substrate,
// top/bottom and bottom/substrate parasitics; split banks retain two top-plate nodes.
// Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani
// and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and
// Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. §II-A–B,
// Figs.1–2, printed pp.2783–2784. Physical PDF pp. 2–3.
// Applies when: Specified charge-scaling architecture and ideal static excitation conditions;
// dynamic reference/switch effects require extensions.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q08

// Theory [Q09; source]: Spiral/chessboard/block choices trade connected components against
// dispersion; the two one-unit members are deliberately only near the common center.
// Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani
// and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and
// Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. §IV-A and
// §IV-C1, printed pp.2788–2790. Physical PDF pp. 7–9.
// Applies when: Identical nonoverlapping grid units; preserve exact electrical counts and quantify
// CC exceptions.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q09

// Theory [Q10; source]: Same-bit components are joined by branch/trunk/bridge routes; top plates
// use graph/MST routing, separately for split-bank nodes.
// Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani
// and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and
// Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. §IV-B–C, printed
// pp.2789–2791. Physical PDF pp. 8–10.
// Applies when: Specified layer/pin/array model; MST wire cost does not prove minimum electrical
// delay.
// Details: ref/Notes/theory-provenance.html#Q10

// Theory [Q11; source]: DNL evaluates adjacent output steps relative to one ideal LSB; INL
// evaluates deviation from the declared ideal transfer.
// Source: Nibedita Karmokar, Arvind K. Sharma, Jitesh Poojary, Meghna Madhusudan, Ramesh Harjani
// and Sachin S. Sapatnekar · IEEE TCAD 42(9), 2023, pp. 2782–2795. Constructive Placement and
// Routing for Common-Centroid Capacitor Arrays in Binary-Weighted and Split DACs. §III,
// Eqs.(17)–(18), printed p.2785. Physical PDF pp. 4.
// Applies when: Record reference convention, code endpoints and normalization; endpoint or
